# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Every `HTTPRoute` on the platform is protected by a platform-managed Web Application Firewall (WAF), applied by default at the Gateway. This includes the OWASP Core Rule Set (CRS) and a list of custom platform rules.

For most applications, this default protection is all you need, and you do not have to do anything else.

See [Web Application Firewall](/documentation/networking/web-application-firewall.html) for how application-specific `EnvoyExtensionPolicy` resources work, why they are not merged with the platform default, and the platform directive they must include to keep the platform-managed rules and exceptions. This page covers how to add your own rules and exceptions on top of that.

## Adding your own rules and exceptions

Adding a rule and adding an exception are essentially the same process: both are added as extra Coraza directives after the platform include directive in your `EnvoyExtensionPolicy`.

### Adding a new rule

Add a `SecRule` stanza after the platform include directive. Rule IDs should be unique and should not overlap with OWASP CRS rule IDs.

The following example adds a new `SecRule` that blocks a specific endpoint:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.envoyproxy.io/v1alpha1
kind: EnvoyExtensionPolicy
metadata:
  name: waf-customization
spec:
  targetRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: HTTPRoute
      name: my-app
  dynamicModule:
    - name: composer
      filterName: coraza-waf
      config:
        directives:
          - Include @coraza.conf
          - SecRuleEngine On
          - Include @crs-setup.conf
          - Include @owasp_crs/*.conf
          - Include /etc/coraza/cp/coraza-cp.conf
          - SecRule REQUEST_URI "@rx /wp-login.php" "id:1000001,phase:1,deny,status:403,log,auditlog,msg:'Block access to legacy admin endpoint'"
```

### Adding a new exception

If a specific request is being blocked but needs to reach your application, first find the rule ID that is blocking it. See [Web Application Firewall Logging and Troubleshooting](/documentation/networking/web-application-firewall-logs-and-trouble.html) for how to identify the triggered rule ID from the WAF logs.

Add an exception in the same way as a rule, using `SecRuleRemoveById` or a more targeted condition. Keep exceptions as narrow as possible and avoid broad removals unless there is a clear and justified need.

```yaml
directives:
  - Include @coraza.conf
  - SecRuleEngine On
  - Include @crs-setup.conf
  - Include @owasp_crs/*.conf
  - Include /etc/coraza/cp/coraza-cp.conf
  - SecRuleRemoveById 920420
```

This disables a specific rule ID for your `HTTPRoute` only. If the exception should only apply in certain circumstances, prefer a scoped rule such as a request URI, header, or argument match rather than removing the rule entirely.

We recommend testing new rules and exceptions using `DetectionOnly` mode before enabling blocking mode. See [Web Application Firewall](/documentation/networking/web-application-firewall.html) for how to use `DetectionOnly` mode and the risks of leaving it enabled.

## Things to consider

- Only create your own `EnvoyExtensionPolicy` if the default platform WAF does not meet your application's needs, for example if you need application-specific rules or exceptions.
- Always include the platform directive shown above, otherwise your policy will be rejected.
- Test any custom rules or exceptions thoroughly, as an overly permissive configuration can weaken your application's protection.
- Check the [Web Application Firewall Logging and Troubleshooting](/documentation/networking/web-application-firewall-logs-and-trouble.html) page if you need to diagnose blocked requests after making changes.

